<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jang Yeong-sil</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1390–after 1442</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jang Yeong-sil was a Korean engineer and inventor of the early Joseon dynasty. Born of low status, he was promoted to high office by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sejong-the-great">King Sejong</a> for his skill. He built astronomical instruments, including armillary spheres, and the self-striking water clock of 1434, and worked on Sejong's programme of scientific instruments that also produced a standard rain gauge in 1441. He was dismissed in 1442 after a royal palanquin he had made broke.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jang-yeong-sil.jpg" alt="Jang Yeong-sil" width="320" height="453" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Himasaram · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jang_Youngsil_3.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
